Factory Method Pattern
Provides a way to encapsulate the instantiation of objects.

The primary intent of the Factory Design Pattern is to define an interface for creating an object, but allow subclasses to alter the type of objects that will be created.

Without Factory Pattern (Factory class) :

Code has direct dependency on concrete classes, making it hard to change, extend, or manage object creation.

With Factory Pattern (Factory class):

Object creation is centralized and abstracted, allowing flexibility in choosing object types and making the code easier to maintain and extend.

Example :

The main (client) uses the ShapeInstanceFactory object to create a shape based on the keyword ("circle", "square", "rectangle").
The Shapeinstancefactory class has a getShapeInstance method that returns one of these Shape types based on the keyword.